# Environment and the two flags that used to be implied by it. Documented here
# for reference, but deliberately left commented out rather than set.
#
# Read this before trusting anything in this file. main.py calls load_dotenv()
# only after the route imports, and the backend reads its configuration in 22
# module-level os.getenv calls that all run during those imports. So on a bare
# `uvicorn main:app` most of this file arrives too late to have any effect, the
# OAuth client IDs included. Pass what you need on the command line, or use
# `just up`, which does it for you. On a droplet none of this bites, because
# compose injects the file via `env_file:` before the process starts.
#
# The three below are commented out for a second reason. Compose injects this
# whole file, so a live value here would outlive the day someone deletes the
# matching line from the overlay, and for the anonymous-admin bypass that
# failure is both silent and open.
#
# To change a deployed box, edit docker-compose.{prod,staging,test}.yml.
#
# RETINA_ENV gates the JWT_SECRET boot check, the RADAR_API_KEY fail-fast and
# the missing-key log levels in radar.py and tcp_handler.py. Deployed:
# `production` on prod, `test` on staging and test.
# RETINA_ENV=dev
#
# AUTH_ALLOW_ANONYMOUS_ADMIN grants every caller anonymous admin when no OAuth
# client is configured. Off unless set to exactly `1`. Deployed: `1` everywhere
# while OAuth is unconfigured (ClickUp 86cb1emcx). Once real credentials are in
# place this must go: it is the whole of the admin boundary. While it is set,
# the admin surfaces that serve TRUE node locations — the ones NODE_FUZZ_MODE
# below exists to keep off the public map — are open to any anonymous caller,
# and the server logs a warning at every boot saying so.
# AUTH_ALLOW_ANONYMOUS_ADMIN=1
#
# SYNTHETIC_FLEET_ENABLED turns on the simulation subsystem: for now the ingest
# write path the fleet POSTs through, and in time the fleet container itself.
# It is the sole gate on that mount, so any environment wanting the subsystem
# has to set it, whatever it is named. Deployed: `1` everywhere, since every
# environment currently runs a fleet, prod included (testmap.retina.fm).
# SYNTHETIC_FLEET_ENABLED=1

# Auth — Google OAuth
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Auth — GitHub OAuth
GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Auth: the key that signs session cookies, the OAuth state parameter and the
# reset/verify tokens. Left empty deliberately. A local run falls back to a
# built-in dev secret, and a deployed box must generate its own:
#
#     printf 'JWT_SECRET=%s\n' "$(openssl rand -hex 32)" >> backend/.env
#
# It used to read `change-me-in-production`, which was harmless while no
# environment was named `production`. Now that one is, core/users.py requires
# the key, and a placeholder published in this repo would have satisfied that
# check with a secret anyone can read. deploy/setup-server.sh generates one for
# a new box, and the production deploy refuses to run without at least 32
# characters here.
JWT_SECRET=

# Auth — comma-separated admin emails
AUTH_ADMIN_EMAILS=admin@retina.fm

# Alerting: webhook for health/critical alerts. Body shape set by ALERT_WEBHOOK_FORMAT below
# (a Slack/Discord/PagerDuty incoming webhook, or a ClickUp chat channel).
ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL=
# Re-notify cooldown per alert type, seconds (default 300)
ALERT_COOLDOWN_S=300
# Sent verbatim as the Authorization header (e.g. a ClickUp personal token, no "Bearer " prefix)
ALERT_WEBHOOK_AUTH=
# Payload shape for ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL: raw (default) or clickup_chat
ALERT_WEBHOOK_FORMAT=
# Labels each alert's "environment" field (production/staging/test). The deployed
# stacks set this in their compose overlay; a bare local run is not expected to.
ALERT_ENVIRONMENT=
# Dead-man's-switch: ping this URL periodically (e.g. a Healthchecks.io check).
# The external service alerts when pings stop. See docs/alerting.md.
HEARTBEAT_URL=

# Health-check thresholds. Both fall back to their default on a value that
# does not parse, rather than stopping the server booting.
# Fleet-average miss rate above which the network counts as blind (default
# 0.98). Set clear of the 72-94% band the network reports when it is working:
# see services/health.py for why an absolute threshold is a stopgap here.
HIGH_MISS_RATE_THRESHOLD=
# Fraction of peak connected nodes below which node_dropout fires (default 0.8).
NODE_DROPOUT_THRESHOLD=

# Mender, read by the in-request device lookup that registration will gate on.
# Same names as node-infra/mender-auto-accept, because it is the same credential
# against the same tenant: one name per secret, so a rotation cannot be applied
# to one component and missed on the other.
#
# MENDER_PAT is a Mender Personal Access Token. Hosted Mender issues no service
# accounts and no per-token scope, so this token inherits everything its creator
# can do, including decommissioning devices and deploying artefacts to the whole
# fleet. retina-server only ever reads devauth with it. See ADR A10 and D26.
#
# This is a bring-up credential, accepted on the terms above so twelve nodes can
# stream by 2026-08-16. The exit is ADR Q1: have mender-auto-accept push the
# accepted-device list here instead, which leaves this file holding no Mender
# credential at all. Keep the file mode at 0600.
MENDER_PAT=
# MENDER_SERVER=https://hosted.mender.io
# MENDER_TIMEOUT_S=3.0

# Node id prefixes that may be force-retired through
# DELETE /api/admin/nodes/{id}/state?force=true. Empty means unrestricted,
# which is what a deployment carrying real receivers wants: a decommissioned
# board also needs force, since nothing else evicts it from the fleet registry.
# Staging sets the E2E prefixes so the suite's teardown cannot reach anything
# else. Read per retirement rather than at import, so unlike most keys in this
# file it does take effect when set here. See ClickUp 86cb5nxvw.
# NODE_FORCE_RETIRE_PREFIXES=

# Multinode solver. SOLVER_WORKERS is both the number of queue-draining threads
# and the size of the process pool they ship the LM solves to; the deployed
# value must fit the container's cpus limit alongside the main process.
#
# SOLVER_RESOLVE_INTERVAL_S is how long an aircraft is not re-solved for.
# Association is rate-limited per node, so every node that sees an aircraft
# emits its own candidate for it and most arrivals are duplicates; this is the
# window that suppresses them. Do not exceed the 60 s multinode_tracks expiry —
# tracks would lapse between solves. 0 turns the suppression off. See
# services/tasks/solver.py and docs/runbook.md's solver_latency_high entry.
# SOLVER_WORKERS=2
# SOLVER_RESOLVE_INTERVAL_S=12

# Detection mirror. Production only. Every accepted v1 detection frame is
# forwarded to another environment's /api/radar/detections/bulk, batched once a
# second, dropped rather than retried when that environment is unreachable, and
# invisible to the nodes either way. DETECTION_MIRROR_KEY must match the
# receiving environment's RADAR_API_KEY. Unset disables the mirror entirely,
# which is where every other environment stands.
# DETECTION_MIRROR_URL=https://test-api.retina.fm
# DETECTION_MIRROR_KEY=
# Node-location privacy. Receivers sit at people's homes, so every payload a
# public client can reach carries a deterministically displaced receiver
# position instead of the true one (services/public_location.py). The pipeline
# still solves against the truth — this is a serialization-boundary change
# only — and transmitters, being licensed broadcast towers, are never moved.
#
# NODE_FUZZ_MODE=off disables it and serves true receiver coordinates. There is
# no reason to set that on anything a stranger can reach.
#
# Each node is displaced along a bearing uniform in [0, 360) by a distance
# uniform in [NODE_FUZZ_MIN_KM, NODE_FUZZ_MAX_KM] — a donut, not a disc, so no
# node is ever left close to home. NODE_FUZZ_MAX_KM is also what clients are
# told the uncertainty is (location_uncertainty_km), so widening it widens the
# disc the map draws.
#
# CHANGING EITHER BOUND MOVES EVERY NODE, and to a new bearing, not just a new
# distance: the bounds in force are part of the HMAC message, so a change of
# bounds is a full re-fuzz that needs no accompanying salt rotation to be safe.
# That is deliberate — re-drawing only the distance would leave every node on
# its old bearing, and anyone holding a position from both frames (the live map
# and the public Parquet archive suffice) could then solve for the true
# receiver exactly. Two consequences worth planning for before you edit these:
# archived Parquet already published in the old frame stays there and no longer
# agrees with the map, and anyone who kept a copy of the old frame gets a
# second, independent sample of each node. Both are the ordinary price of a
# re-fuzz; neither is a reason to change the bounds without changing the frame.
#
# NODE_FUZZ_SALT keys the HMAC that picks each node's offset. Set it and the
# offsets are reproducible across environments and rebuilds; leave it unset and
# the server generates one on first boot and persists it under
# backend/data/runtime/node_fuzz_salt, which keeps offsets stable across
# restarts but makes them local to that deployment's volume. CHANGING IT MOVES
# EVERY NODE ON THE MAP — treat it as a secret, back it up with the volume, and
# do not rotate it casually.
# NODE_FUZZ_MODE=on
# NODE_FUZZ_MIN_KM=0.5
# NODE_FUZZ_MAX_KM=1.0
# NODE_FUZZ_SALT=
